cenarios. The underlying approximation is found by our relating these eigenvalues to tunneling probabilities in an associated quantum-mechanical Hamiltonian, which is generalized here to cope with deformed geometries by a semiclassical transformation of the problem to the traditionally studied rectangular one.\n\n### Physics Subject Headings (PhySH)\n\n  * Classical optics\n  * Information & communication theory\n  * Near-field optics\n  * Lenses\n  * Wireless communication networks\n  * Mathematical physics methods\n  * Phase space methods\n\n## Article Text\n\n### I. INTRODUCTION\n\nThe advancement of wireless communication towards sixth-generation technologies is placing increased importance on massive multiple-input–multiple-output (MIMO) systems [Button: [1–6]]. Innovations such as reconfigurable intelligent surfaces [Button: [7]] demand, in particular, a characterization of communication that goes beyond simpler models using free-space and far-field assumptions to treat arrays that are electrically large and placed within confined spaces.\n\nThis motivates us in this paper to extend explicit analytical models, previously developed in a form that applies to far-field communication, so that they apply also to the more complicated geometries encountered in such systems. The analysis is focused on an evaluation of the eigenvalues of the integral operator defined by\n\n(1)\n\nwhich can be directly motivated in the far-field setting and whose eigenvalues we showed in a previous publication [Button: [8]] to allow interpretation as tunneling probabilities in an analog quantum-mechanical problem. This allowed us, for example, to uniformly extend a semiclassical approximation of the eigenvalues describing a transition from values near unity to values near zero. We extend that approach in this paper to treat the wider class of integral equations that arise in models of near-field communication in two-dimensional (2D) MIMO systems, for which Eq. [Button: (1)] describes a far-field limit. The underlying mathematics also addresses a basic quantum-mechanical problem of characterizing the extent to which states can be localized within a finite patch in phase space.\n\nFor near-field communication, one can define an analogous integral operator projecting onto a region of the phase plane that is similarly filled by rays connecting the transmitter to the receiver, but this region is no longer a parallelogram. It may have curved edges and a height that varies across its width, for example. We are no longer able to find an exact commuting differential equation in this case. However, we show that a deformation of the Hamiltonian in Eq. [Button: (1)] provides the leading term in an asymptotic expansion (in powers of ) that serves the same purpose. Furthermore, while subsequent terms in this expansion are needed to uniformly approximate the eigenvalues across the entire spectrum, the leading term on its own suffices to describe the eigenvalues in the most important transition region.\n\nFinally, we outline the content of this paper. We begin in Sec. [Button: II] by providing the setting and notation for the deformed geometry of interest in this paper, first from the perspective of ray tracing and the corresponding phase space and then by describing the operator arising from a scalar wave model whose eigenvalues we seek to approximate. In Sec. [Button: III] we describe the coordinate transformation that allows us to map the deformed problem back to the standard case treated in Ref. [Button: [8]]. In Sec. [Button: IV] we calculate explicitly the operator on wave functions defined semiclassically by this coordinate transformation and use it to calculate eigenfunctions. Conclusions are given in Sec. [Button: V].\n\n### II. DEFORMED FILTERING OPERATOR FOR NEAR-FIELD COMMUNICATION\n\nWe describe in this section how the setup in Ref. [Button: [8]] is adjusted to treat the case of near-field communication (see Fig. [Button: 1]), first from the perspective of phase space and then from the perspective of a scalar wave model whose eigenvalues provide the quantities of interest in this paper.\n\nFIG. 1.\n\nImage\n\nThe geometry assumed and the coordinates used to characterize rays in phase space, with position coordinates in (a) and the incidence angles used to define momenta [through Eq. [Button: (2)]] in (b).\n\n#### A. Phase-space geometry for near-field communication\n\nAs in Ref. [Button: [8]], we use a 2D scalar model of signal propagation, whose phase-space representation is illustrated in Fig. [Button: 2]. Explicit calculations are performed for line-of-sight communication between parallel, straight boundaries, but the underlying semiclassical results in this paper are easily adapted to other 2D geometries, under assumptions that lead to locally constant phase-space densities. The simpler geometry in Fig. [Button: 1] is used in numerical illustrations because it allows us to present ray lengths and other dynamical properties in explicit analytical forms.\n\nFIG. 2.\n\nImage\n\n(a) and (b)the corresponding . This is for the geometry and .\n\nWe assume that the receiving and transmitting antennae are continuously distributed over intervals and , respectively, in their respective boundaries and that these boundaries are a distance apart. Rays intersecting receiving and transmitting boundaries are placed on phase planes with canonical coordinates , where is defined with the use of the incidence angle by\n\n(2)\n\nFor line-of-sight geometry, the “momentum” variable is conserved, and a ray leaving the transmitting boundary at with momentum arrives at the receiving boundary with momentum at position\n\n(3)\n\nWe denote the corresponding map by\n\nWe adopt the convention in this paper that coordinates on the transmitter side are primed and those on the receiver side are unprimed.\n\nBy an abuse of notation, we use the symbols and to denote also the strips defined by and in their respective phase planes. #### B. Wave 