ays arriving in from . It is represented explicitly by the kernel\n\n(11)\n\nwhere\n\nwhere is defined in Eq. [Button: (7)]. We are not able to reduce this integral to a particularly simpler form so we leave it as this general expression. The operator is easily approximated numerically though, through a sequence of appropriate Fourier transforms and with the use of Eq. [Button: (6)].\n\n#### C. Eigenvalues\n\nWe now seek eigensolutions, denoted\n\nThe simpler integral operator defined in Eq. [Button: (1)] can be solved efficiently with the use of projection onto a basis of Legendre polynomials [Button: [17,][Button: 18]]. There is no corresponding exact theory for the general version [Button: (11)], and we instead find its eigensolutions numerically by brute-force evaluation of by discrete Fourier transforms approximating it.\n\nAn example of one such set of numerically calculated eigenvalues is shown in Fig. [Button: 3(a)] as filled circles, showing a characteristic behavior of being close to unity for small , undergoing a transition around to exponential-like decay for larger values of . These eigenvalues were calculated for a geometry of directly opposed arrays, with limits , which is like the case treated in Ref. [Button: [8]] but with a sufficiently small value of the separation that the far-field conditions assumed there do not hold. Nevertheless, these eigenvalues are well approximated by the tunneling model in Ref. [Button: [8]], shown as a continuous curve: this was calculated after replacement of the parameter used in that paper by\n\n(12)\n\nwhich is adjusted to the current near-field geometry, of which we need to know only the area of intersection defined in Eq. [Button: (4)]. We chose in this illustration so that , , and . As a result, labeling the eigenvalues with a counting index , we find that the eigenvalue with (corresponding to ) lies in the middle of the transition, with . Despite the significant deviation of this near-field geometry from the assumption in Ref. [Button: [8]] of a patch with constant height in phase space, the tunneling approximation derived there provides an excellent description of the eigenvalues across the transition around . The rest of the paper is devoted to understanding why this works.\n\nFIG. 3.\n\nImage\n\nEven for the near-field case and , the tunneling model derived in Ref. [Button: [8]] provides a good description of the eigenvalues around the transition region, where ( in this illustration). Filled circles represent a brute-force calculation for the near-field problem, and lie on top of crossed circles, representing an equivalent (i.e., having the same value of ) linearized problem treated in Ref. [Button: [8]]. The various continuous curves describe approximations described in more detail in Ref. [Button: [8]] and in Sec. [Button: III].\n\nThe complex tunneling orbit whose action determines decay rates is currently defined after mapping the phase space around the intersection onto a canonical, rectangular, and intrinsically 2D geometry—a means of constructing this orbit intrinsically in terms of the original phase-space geometry would provide a promising route to understanding the higher-dimensional case, but we do not currently know how to achieve this.\n\n### ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\n\nThis work was supported by the European Union Horizon 2020 RISE-6G project (Grant No. 101017011) and by the Royal Society Industry Fellowship (Grant No. INF\\R2\\192066).\n\n### Appendices\n\n### APPENDIX: DERIVATION OF RECTIFYING COORDINATES\n\nTo derive the rectifying coordinate given in Eq. [Button: (24)], we use as a Hamiltonian to generate trajectories parametrized by “time” according to\n\n(A1)\n\nand\n\n(A2)\n\nsubject to the initial condition\n\n(A3)\n\nNote that differentiation of Eq. [Button: (22)] gives\n\n(A4)\n\nUsing the fact that is preserved on these trajectories, and inverting the second of Hamilton’s equations, Eq. [Button: (A2)], we get\n\nwhere the subscript on the partial derivat